{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the Old Bridge of Mostar was a violation of the prohibition of wanton destruction of civilian objects because the bridge was not a legitimate military objective and the attack was disproportionate to any anticipated military advantage.",
      "reasoning": "International humanitarian law requires that attacks be directed only at military objectives and that any incidental civilian damage not be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated (principles of distinction and proportionality). The bridge, while used sporadically for limited combat activities, was primarily a civilian cultural monument whose destruction would not yield a decisive military benefit, as the Trial Chamber found only a modest logistical impact. The resulting total isolation of civilians and the psychological impact far outweighed any minor advantage, indicating the attack was wanton and not justified by military necessity.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the bridge constituted devastation not justified by military necessity under customary international humanitarian law protecting cultural property.",
      "reasoning": "Customary IHL, reflected in the 1954 Hague Convention and its protocols, obliges parties to refrain from targeting cultural property unless it makes an effective contribution to military operations and its destruction offers a definite military advantage. The bridge, a sixteenth‑century heritage structure, did not meet this threshold; its loss served primarily symbolic and psychological purposes rather than a concrete military necessity, making the attack unlawful.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The HVO’s intent to sap the morale of the Muslim civilian population demonstrates that the attack was directed at civilians, thereby constituting a war crime of attacking civilian objects.",
      "reasoning": "The intent behind an attack is a key element in determining its legality. International jurisprudence (e.g., ICTY jurisprudence on attacks motivated by intimidation) holds that when the primary purpose of an attack is to terrorise or demoralise a civilian population, the attack is unlawful regardless of any incidental military use of the target. The Trial Chamber’s finding that the bridge was destroyed to sap civilian morale shows the attack was aimed at civilians, violating the prohibition on attacks against civilian objects.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Commanders of the HVO who ordered or failed to prevent the destruction of the bridge are criminally liable under the doctrine of command responsibility.",
      "reasoning": "Command responsibility attaches when a superior knew or had reason to know of the commission of a war crime by subordinates and failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures to prevent it or to punish the perpetrators. The commanders who authorized the tank fire either knew or should have known that the bridge was a civilian cultural monument and that its destruction was not militarily justified, satisfying the requisite knowledge element, and they did not intervene to stop the attack, thus meeting the criteria for liability.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
